 Amazon RDS: Managed Relational Databases on AWS

Managing databases manually (patching, backups, scaling, failover) is time-consuming and risky.
Amazon RDS (Relational Database Service) removes that operational burden and lets you focus on applications instead of managing DB infrastructure.

RDS is one of the most important database services in AWS — and commonly discussed in interviews.
Here’s what you need to know 👇



🔹 What is Amazon RDS?

RDS is a fully managed relational database service that handles:

Automated backups

Patch management

High availability

Monitoring

Multi-AZ failover

Storage scaling


You simply choose the engine, instance class, and storage — AWS manages everything else.



🔹 Supported Database Engines

RDS supports popular relational databases:

Amazon Aurora

MySQL

PostgreSQL

MariaDB

Oracle

SQL Server


💡 Tip: If you need high performance + auto-scaling, look at Aurora.



🔹 Key Features of RDS

🛡️ 1. Multi-AZ Deployment

Provides automatic failover to a standby instance in another AZ.
Best for production-grade HA.

🔄 2. Automated Backups & Snapshots

Daily backups

Point-in-time restore

Manual snapshots retained until deleted


📏 3. Read Replicas

Used to offload read-heavy workloads and scale read performance.

📈 4. Storage Auto Scaling

Automatically increases storage when running out — avoids “database full” outages.

🔍 5. Monitoring & Insights

CloudWatch metrics

Performance Insights

Enhanced Monitoring


Great for troubleshooting slow queries or CPU spikes.



